RAFT\n4. Multi-turn alignment: TRUTH DECAY, debate-based alignment, recursive reward modeling\n\n**References (22 total):** Burns et al. 2023, Lyu et al. 2024, Perez et al. 2023, Sharma et al. 2024, Turner et al. 2023, Wei et al. 2024, Zou et al. 2023, Shao et al. 2024, Chen et al. 2024, Liu et al. 2024, Li et al. 2024, Rimsky et al. 2024, Marks et al. 2024, Azaria & Mitchell 2023, Rafailov et al. 2024, Li et al. 2024 (SYCON), Zheng et al. 2024, Hendrycks et al. 2021, Sun et al. 2024, Bowman 2023, Zhong et al. 2024, Hu et al. 2022, Park et al. 2024, Feng et al. 2024, Yang et al. 2025\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Discriminative signal in activation space for sycophancy (AUROC 0.843)\n2. Representation-level continuous rewards improve GRPO (RC-AUC 0.738 vs 0.641), directional projection as one instantiation\n3. RC-AUC evaluation framework\n4. Analysis: R1-R3 disagreement, direction stability, turn-depth effects, cross-model transfer\n\n## Your Review Task\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is extracting d_syc? (RepE, CCS, Activation Addition already establish linear directions in activation spaces)\n- How novel is integrating representation-level signals into GRPO rewards?\n- The paper acknowledges the direction extraction follows \"established methodology\" — is the integration itself sufficiently novel?\n- Given that Continuous Probe matches DC-GRPO's performance, what is the unique contribution of directional projection specifically?\n- Is RC-AUC a meaningful methodological contribution or just a straightforward AUC application?\n- How does this compare to \"Pressure, What Pressure?\" (Liu et al. 2024) which already decomposes GRPO rewards?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are the key prior works adequately covered?\n- Missing references? Consider: knowledge-prediction gap work (Feng et al. 2024 is cited), internal state detection (Azaria & Mitchell 2023 is cited), GRPO/RLHF training literature\n- Is the positioning fair to prior work (especially ACT, \"Pressure, What Pressure?\", and Linear Probe Penalties)?\n- Does the paper adequately discuss what is known vs what is new about linear directions in LLMs?\n- 22 references — is this sufficient for a top venue? (typical is 30-50)\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n- The paper's honest framing (\"representation-level signals, not directional geometry\") is scientifically commendable. But does this honesty undermine the paper's own contribution?\n- Is the paper effectively a \"negative result\" for directional projection but \"positive result\" for representation-level rewards? If so, is it framed appropriately?\n- The title emphasizes \"Direction-Contrastive\" but the main finding is that direction doesn't matter much. Is this misleading?\n- How well does the paper differentiate from simply using a probe-based reward (Park et al. 2024)?\n\n### 4. Significance and Impact\n- Who would use this work and why?\n- Is the contribution sufficient for a top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate)?\n- Is the RC-AUC metric likely to be adopted by the community?\n- Does the turn-depth analysis provide genuinely new insights?\n\n### 5. Presentation Quality\n- Is the story coherent from Abstract through Conclusion?\n- Does the paper successfully navigate the tension between \"we proposed directional projection\" and \"directional projection doesn't clearly outperform simpler probes\"?\n- Any AI writing style issues? (dashes, -ing phrases, filler words)\n\n### 6. Top Weaknesses\nList 3-5 weaknesses with specific evidence.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\nList 3-5 specific questions.\n\n### 8. Overall Assessment\nScore on 1-6 (1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept) with justification for a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\nFormat with clear headers. Be specific and cite evidence."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UhqgWBsMnZsYF3gAxTbLm8","session_id":"ab47c9b5-80cc-4b25-a0ea-3684b0ab8990","uuid":"b3221cd6-ca0b-45ac-970a-9d9449c1cb58","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:49:29.329Z"}
