{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GhpsP1Yoxbs9GdFZbak17s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015yazqvNnkwG6QNRMGJNgcA","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T125925/median/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2183,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19798,"cache_read_input_tokens":85921,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19798,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UhqgWBsMnZsYF3gAxTbLm8","session_id":"ab47c9b5-80cc-4b25-a0ea-3684b0ab8990","uuid":"f6e98d3c-27a0-493d-a2ad-89e0a95731cc"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015yazqvNnkwG6QNRMGJNgcA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcommand{\\dsyc}{$d_{\\text{syc}}$}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\dgen}{$d_{\\text{gen}}$}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\rcauc}{\\text{RC-AUC}}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy Calibration:\\\\Representation-Level Rewards Break the Revision Double-Bind}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t% ============================================================\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\t% ============================================================\n29\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) face a persistent tension in multi-turn dialogue: anti-sycophancy training methods suppress \\emph{all} opinion changes indiscriminately, blocking both unwarranted capitulation and legitimate belief revision. We investigate whether the policy model's own activation space encodes a discriminative signal between these two behaviors and whether that signal can serve as a reward component during Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). Using style-controlled contrastive pairs, we extract a linear direction \\dsyc{} from mid-layer activations (AUROC 0.843, 95\\% CI [0.812, 0.874]) and incorporate it into a three-component reward: directional projection (R1), rank-based contrastive (R2), and NLI behavioral (R3). The resulting Direction-Contrastive GRPO (DC-GRPO) reduces sycophantic capitulation (NoF 0.218 vs.\\ 0.291 for behavioral-only GRPO, $p$=0.021) while maintaining warranted revision rates (WRR 0.584 vs.\\ 0.558). Controlled comparisons with binary and continuous probe baselines reveal that the improvement stems primarily from representation-level continuous reward signals rather than directional geometry alone. We propose Revision-Capitulation AUC (RC-AUC), a composite metric that captures the sycophancy-revision tradeoff; DC-GRPO achieves 0.738 RC-AUC compared to 0.641 for behavioral-only training ($p$=0.008). Cross-model evaluation on Llama-3-8B confirms framework generalizability (RC-AUC 0.698), though direction vectors are model-specific (cross-model cosine similarity 0.31).\n30\t\\end{abstract}\n31\t\n32\t% ============================================================\n33\t\\section{Introduction}\n34\t\\label{sec:intro}\n35\t% ============================================================\n36\t\n37\tMulti-turn dialogue presents a fundamental challenge for LLM alignment: models must resist unwarranted social pressure while remaining open to legitimate corrections. When a user provides valid counter-evidence, a well-calibrated model should revise its position; when a user merely expresses disagreement without substantive grounds, the model should maintain its answer. Existing anti-sycophancy training conflates these two cases, producing models that are either too compliant or too stubborn.\n38\t\n39\tThis conflation has measurable consequences. Prior work has documented accuracy degradation of up to 47\\% across dialogue turns as models lock into incorrect positions. Undifferentiated activation consistency methods penalize all behavioral changes equally, suppressing legitimate revision alongside sycophantic capitulation. Behavioral reward decomposition via Natural Language Inference (NLI) captures surface-level inconsistency but cannot detect cases where a model's internal reasoning has already shifted toward agreement while its output maintains a facade of independence.\n40\t\n41\tWe hypothesize that the policy model's activation space contains a signal that distinguishes sycophantic capitulation from genuine belief revision. Building on Representation Engineering, which established that linear directions in activation space encode high-level concepts, we extract a direction \\dsyc{} from style-controlled multi-turn contrastive pairs. The key contribution is not the direction extraction itself, which follows established methodology, but its integration as a continuous reward signal within GRPO training.\n42\t\n43\tWe propose Direction-Contrastive GRPO (DC-GRPO), a training framework with three reward components: directional projection on \\dsyc{} (R1), rank-based contrastive scoring (R2), and NLI behavioral scoring (R3). Through systematic comparison with four baselines (behavioral-only GRPO, undifferentiated activation consistency, binary probe penalty, and continuous probe reward), we find that representation-level continuous signals consistently improve the sycophancy-revision tradeoff, and that directional projection is one effective instantiation of this principle. We also introduce Revision-Capitulation AUC (RC-AUC), a composite evaluation metric designed to capture this tradeoff in a single score.\n44\t\n45\tOur contributions are:\n46\t\\begin{itemize}\n47\t    \\item We demonstrate that the policy model's activation space contains a discriminative signal for sycophantic capitulation (AUROC 0.843 at layer 18), with signal emergence at mid-layers and degradation under strong counter-evidence (Section~\\ref{sec:calibration}).\n48\t    \\item We show that incorporating representation-level continuous reward signals into GRPO improves the sycophancy-revision tradeoff: DC-GRPO achieves 0.738 RC-AUC vs.\\ 0.641 for behavioral-only training, with directional projection serving as one principled instantiation (Section~\\ref{sec:main_results}).\n49\t    \\item We propose the RC-AUC evaluation framework, which jointly captures capitulation reduction and revision preservation, and validate it across six training configurations (Section~\\ref{sec:main_results}).\n50\t    \\item We provide analysis of reward signal disagreement patterns, direction stability during training, turn-depth effects, and cross-model transferability (Sections~\\ref{sec:analysis}--\\ref{sec:cross_model}).\n51\t\\end{itemize}\n52\t\n53\t% ============================================================\n54\t\\section{Related Work}\n55\t\\label{sec:related}\n56\t% ============================================================\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Sycophancy in LLMs.}\n59\tSycophancy, the tendency of LLMs to agree with user opinions regardless of factual accuracy, has been documented across model families and scales. Multi-turn sycophancy compounds the problem through commitment escalation: models that capitulate to social pressure in early turns become increasingly entrenched in incorrect positions. SYCON Bench and SycophancyEval provide evaluation protocols for this phenomenon, though both focus primarily on surface behavioral consistency rather than internal model states.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Anti-sycophancy training.}\n62\tSeveral approaches attempt to reduce sycophancy through training interventions. Behavioral Consistency Training (BCT) and Activation Consistency Training (ACT) penalize changes in model behavior or activations when faced with user pressure, but apply this penalty indiscriminately to all revisions. ``Pressure, What Pressure?'' decomposes GRPO rewards using NLI-based behavioral scoring, enabling more targeted optimization. SMART introduces structured multi-agent debate to identify and penalize sycophantic outputs. All of these methods operate on behavioral signals without access to the model's internal representation of the sycophancy-revision distinction.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Representation Engineering.}\n65\tRepresentation Engineering (RepE) demonstrated that high-level concepts (honesty, fairness, harmlessness) are encoded as linear directions in LLM activation spaces. Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS) finds truth directions via unsupervised consistency objectives. Activation Addition steers model behavior at inference time by adding directional vectors to activations. Linear Probe Penalties use binary classifiers on the reward model's activations to shape training. CRAFT validates hidden-state GRPO for safety alignment using domain-general prototype clustering. Our work extends this line by applying directional decomposition specifically to the sycophancy-revision distinction in multi-turn settings.\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Multi-turn alignment.}\n68\tMulti-turn RLHF remains understudied compared to single-turn alignment. TRUTH DECAY documents the compounding effect of commitment across turns. Recent work on debate-based alignment and recursive reward modeling addresses multi-turn consistency, but not the specific sycophancy-revision tradeoff. Our turn-depth analysis (Section~\\ref{sec:turn_depth}) provides evidence that representation-level signals become more valuable as dialogue depth increases.\n69\t\n70\t% ============================================================\n71\t\\section{Method}\n72\t\\label{sec:method}\n73\t% ============================================================\n74\t\n75\t\\subsection{Direction Extraction}\n76\t\\label{sec:direction}\n77\t\n78\tWe extract a linear direction \\dsyc{} in the policy model's activation space that discriminates sycophantic capitulation from genuine belief revision. The calibration dataset consists of 200 style-controlled contrastive pairs stratified by evidence strength: 80 weak, 60 medium, and 60 strong. Each pair presents identical surface-level behavior (the model revises its answer) but differs in whether the revision is warranted by the evidence provided.\n79\t\n80\tStyle control is critical: without it, the direction captures superficial features (hedging language, confidence markers) rather than the underlying cognitive distinction. Our v2.1 protocol matches pairs on response length, hedging frequency, and lexical diversity, isolating the representation-level signal.\n81\t\n82\tFor a given layer $\\ell$, let $\\mathbf{h}^+_i$ and $\\mathbf{h}^-_i$ denote the hidden states at the assistant's first token position for genuine revision and sycophantic capitulation examples, respectively. The direction is computed as:\n83\t\\begin{equation}\n84\t    d_{\\text{syc}}^{(\\ell)} = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N} (\\mathbf{h}^-_i - \\mathbf{h}^+_i)\n85\t\\end{equation}\n86\tWe select the layer $\\ell^*$ that maximizes 5-fold cross-validated AUROC on held-out pairs. The projection of a new activation $\\mathbf{h}$ onto the normalized direction $\\hat{d}_{\\text{syc}}$ provides a continuous sycophancy score:\n87\t\\begin{equation}\n88\t    s_{\\text{syc}}(\\mathbf{h}) = \\mathbf{h} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{\\text{syc}}^{(\\ell^*)}\n89\t\\end{equation}\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{DC-GRPO Training}\n92\t\\label{sec:dcgrpo}\n93\t\n94\tDC-GRPO augments Group Relative Policy Optimization with a three-component reward function:\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{R1: Directional projection reward.} Given a response activation $\\mathbf{h}$, the directional reward is:\n97\t\\begin{equation}\n98\t    R_1 = -\\sigma(\\alpha \\cdot s_{\\text{syc}}(\\mathbf{h}))\n99\t\\end{equation}\n100\twhere $\\sigma$ is the sigmoid function and $\\alpha$ is a temperature parameter. Higher projection onto \\dsyc{} (more sycophantic) yields lower reward."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UhqgWBsMnZsYF3gAxTbLm8","session_id":"ab47c9b5-80cc-4b25-a0ea-3684b0ab8990","uuid":"62527cce-84e5-441c-acda-18fd40cfabff","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:50:08.692Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae7d15c37902555f6","tool_use_id":"toolu_01GoLvbPGeLiJ1EuonLwnp8W","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Method + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":125567,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":135286},"uuid":"fe8ff2a7-a043-4868-bdb4-52d1e3f1096e","session_id":"ab47c9b5-80cc-4b25-a0ea-3684b0ab8990"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GoLvbPGeLiJ1EuonLwnp8W","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper across all three dream tiers. Let me produce the structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methodology Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n**Paper:** Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy Calibration: Representation-Level Rewards Break the Revision Double-Bind\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodology Soundness\n\n**Direction extraction (d_syc):** The method is well-motivated and technically sound in principle. Computing the mean difference vector from style-controlled contrastive pairs follows established RepE methodology (Zou et al., 2023). The style-control protocol (v2.1) is crucial and the paper documents its necessity through the progression from v1 (trivial confound, AUROC=1.0 at Layer 0) to v2 to v2.1. The mid-layer emergence pattern (L0-4 near random, peak at L18/L19) is consistent with prior work on linear representations and adds credibility.\n\nHowever, I have two concerns:\n\n(a) The direction is a simple mean difference vector, not PCA or any robust estimator. With 200 pairs, outliers could disproportionately influence the direction. The paper does not report any analysis of direction robustness to outlier removal or alternative extraction methods.\n\n(b) The evidence-strength stratification results raise a subtle issue. In the **median tier**, AUROC *decreases* with stronger evidence (weak 0.871 > medium 0.832 > strong 0.809), which makes intuitive sense: under strong evidence, genuine revision and sycophantic capitulation produce similar internal states because the model genuinely processes the evidence. But in the **best tier**, the ordering reverses (weak 0.863 < medium 0.894 < strong 0.921). These are contradictory predictions for the same experiment, and the correct ordering is theoretically ambiguous. The paper should justify why the observed ordering is expected rather than assuming one pattern and retroactively rationalizing it.\n\n**Three-component reward (R1, R2, R3):** The design is reasonable but under-specified in critical areas:\n\n- **R2 is vaguely described.** \"Rank-based contrastive\" is mentioned but the mathematical formulation is not provided. How exactly are within-batch responses ranked? What is the reward function over ranks? This is a reproducibility concern.\n- **Reward weight defaults (w1=0.3, w2=0.2, w3=0.5)** lack theoretical justification. The sensitivity analysis shows they work well empirically, but the initial selection appears arbitrary. The paper would be stronger if the choice were motivated, e.g., by a development set or by analysis of component magnitudes.\n- **R1-R3 interaction:** The ablation cascade (R3-only -> R1-only -> R1+R2 -> R1+R2+R3) is informative and well-structured. The non-trivial contribution of each component is convincingly demonstrated.\n\n**RC-AUC metric:** The concept of plotting WRR against (1-NoF) across thresholds is reasonable and addresses a real gap. However, the paper does not clearly specify what \"decision thresholds\" are swept to generate the curve. Is it a threshold on the total reward? On a confidence score? This definition is critical for the metric to be meaningful and reproducible. Without it, RC-AUC remains ambiguous.\n\n**Recalibration mechanism:** Every 500 steps with a 0.70 cosine threshold. The mechanism was never triggered in the best-case scenario (cosine stayed at 0.881). In the median case, cosine drops to 0.76 and is borderline. The 0.70 threshold seems arbitrary and may be too permissive. Had the paper used a 0.80 threshold, recalibration would have been triggered, potentially changing results. The choice is not well justified.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**Baselines:** The baseline set is comprehensive and well-chosen: Vanilla, R3-only (behavioral), ACT (undifferentiated activation consistency), Binary Probe, and Continuous Probe. All are trained with the same hyperparameters except the reward function, which is the correct experimental design. The inclusion of Continuous Probe is particularly important and was clearly added in response to internal review (D008).\n\n**3 seeds and statistical power:** This is the paper's most significant weakness. With only 3 seeds:\n- Paired t-tests have 2 degrees of freedom, making p-values unreliable.\n- Several claims are made based on p-values that are borderline. DC-GRPO vs R3-only NoF: p=0.021 (significant), WRR: p=0.148 (NOT significant). The paper claims DC-GRPO \"improves warranted revision rate by 12.2 pp\" but this improvement is not statistically significant.\n- Cohen's d calculations with n=3 have massive uncertainty.\n\nThe fact that the paper uses paired t-tests with n=3 and reports p-values to three decimal places creates a false impression of precision. With 3 observations, the t-distribution is very heavy-tailed, and small differences in the third seed can flip significance. I would require at least 5 seeds for the primary claims, preferably 10.\n\n**The critical DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe comparison:** This is the most important analysis in the paper and the numbers differ dramatically between tiers:\n- **Best tier:** DC-GRPO significantly outperforms Continuous Probe (NoF -7.9pp, p=0.014). The paper claims directional geometry is the primary driver.\n- **Median tier:** DC-GRPO does NOT significantly outperform Continuous Probe (NoF -0.6pp, p=0.381; RC-AUC +0.7pp, p=0.318). The improvement comes from continuous signals, not directional geometry.\n\nThe median-tier result is honest and informative, and the paper (in its median version) appropriately adjusts its claims. The best-tier version claims something stronger than what the median tier supports. The question is which outcome is more likely given the experimental design. With AUROC 0.843 on 200 pairs (median), a 7.9pp gap between directional projection and continuous probe probability (best) seems optimistic. The continuous probe and the directional projection both operate on the same underlying information (model activations at the same layer), so large divergence requires the geometric structure to carry substantial independent information.\n\n**Calibration data:** 200 pairs is adequate for direction extraction but marginal for strong statistical claims. The 95% CI width varies between 0.034 (best) and 0.062 (median). A CI width of 0.062 means the true AUROC could plausibly be anywhere from 0.812 to 0.874, which spans a meaningful range. The paper should acknowledge that 400-500 pairs would substantially strengthen confidence in the calibration signal.\n\n**Cross-model evaluation:** Testing on only 2 models (both 8B parameters) is a clear limitation. The Llama-3-8B training was shorter (3000 vs 6500-8000 steps), making direct comparison unfair. The cross-model direction cosine of 0.31-0.34 is low, confirming directions are model-specific. This weakens claims of generality. The paper should test on at least one model from a different parameter scale (e.g., 1.5B or 70B) to assess whether the phenomenon scales.\n\n**Direction stability:** AUROC drops from 0.843 to 0.791 (median) over 8000 steps, approaching the 0.80 gate. This is a genuine concern. If training were extended (e.g., to 12000 steps for better convergence), AUROC could drop below 0.80, at which point the R1 signal becomes unreliable. The paper does not investigate what happens when the recalibration threshold is actually triggered.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Claims vs Evidence\n\n**Claim 1 (discriminative signal):** Supported. AUROC 0.843 (median) at L18 with style-controlled data is a genuine finding. The mid-layer emergence pattern is convincing. However, \"discriminates\" is too strong for AUROC 0.843 with CI lower bound at 0.812. More appropriate: \"the activation space contains a moderately discriminative signal.\"\n\n**Claim 2 (DC-GRPO effectiveness):** Partially supported. DC-GRPO consistently outperforms R3-only and ACT. The critical question is whether the advantage is attributable to directional geometry or to continuous representation-level signals generally. The median-tier results (DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe not significant) suggest the latter. The best-tier claims of significant directional advantage (p=0.014) should be treated cautiously given n=3.\n\n**Claim 3 (R1-R3 disagreement):** The paper appropriately downgrades this to an analysis subsection rather than a formal contribution. The disagreement rate varies between 7.6% (median) and 14.2% (best). The structured patterns (increase with turn depth, concentration in medium evidence) are informative regardless of the absolute rate. The paper does not provide human validation of whether flagged cases are truly \"masked sycophancy\" versus noise.\n\n**RC-AUC contribution:** As an evaluation metric, RC-AUC fills a genuine gap. However, the metric needs clearer formal specification (what thresholds are swept) and external validation (correlation with human judgments). Without these, it risks the \"strongest results on self-invented metric\" criticism.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n\nThe paper is generally well-organized with a logical progression: direction extraction, main results, ablations, analysis. Tables are informative with appropriate standard deviations reported. The Related Work section correctly positions the work within RepE/CCS and distinguishes it from CRAFT and ACT.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The abstract in the best tier over-claims (\"AUROC 0.891,\" \"7.9 pp improvement with p=0.014 against Continuous Probe\") while the median tier is more honest. The actual experimental results will determine which is appropriate.\n- R2 reward component is inadequately formalized. The \"rank-based contrastive\" description is insufficient for reproduction.\n- The paper does not report the computational overhead of DC-GRPO vs R3-only training (direction extraction, recalibration every 500 steps, activation extraction per batch). This is practically important.\n- No human evaluation of any outputs. WRR and NoF depend on automated classification of whether revision is \"warranted\" or capitulation is \"sycophantic.\" The accuracy of these classifiers is not validated.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Top Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Insufficient seeds for statistical claims (Critical).** All statistical tests use n=3 seeds with paired t-tests (2 df). This is severely underpowered. The WRR comparison between DC-GRPO and R3-only (p=0.148) already fails significance. With more seeds, some currently \"significant\" results may fail, and the critical DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe comparison (which determines the core novelty claim) is extremely sensitive to noise. The p=0.014 in the best tier vs p=0.381 in the median tier illustrates this fragility.\n\n**W2. Directional geometry vs continuous signals not clearly separated.** The median-tier results show DC-GRPO and Continuous Probe are statistically indistinguishable. This means the paper's title claim (\"representation-level rewards break the double-bind\") is more accurately about continuous activation-space signals, not specifically about directional projection. If the final experiments confirm the median scenario, the novelty is substantially reduced: a logistic regression probability works equally well.\n\n**W3. Limited model diversity.** Only two 8B models from different families, with unequal training budgets. No exploration of parameter scale effects. Cross-model direction cosine of 0.31 suggests the specific findings may not generalize.\n\n**W4. RC-AUC under-specified.** The paper's proposed metric lacks formal definition of the threshold sweep procedure and has no correlation with human judgments. The strongest results (RC-AUC 0.738 vs 0.641) are on this self-defined metric.\n\n**W5. No human validation.** Neither the calibration data labels (sycophantic vs genuine) nor the evaluation metrics (NoF, WRR) are validated against human judgments. The entire pipeline assumes the automated classifiers are correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe comparison is the crux of the paper's novelty claim. With only 3 seeds, what is the statistical power to detect a 7.9pp difference at alpha=0.05? Please report power analysis and consider whether 5+ seeds would change conclusions.\n\n**Q2.** The evidence-strength stratification of AUROC has a theoretically ambiguous direction (should strong evidence yield higher or lower AUROC?). Can you provide a formal argument for why the observed ordering is expected, rather than post-hoc rationalization?\n\n**Q3.** What is the formal definition of R2 (rank-based contrastive reward)? The current description is insufficient for reproduction. Please provide the mathematical formulation.\n\n**Q4.** How would you respond to the possibility that a Continuous Probe achieves equivalent results (as in the median scenario)? Would you adjust the contribution claims to focus on the evaluation framework and multi-turn analysis rather than directional geometry?\n\n**Q5.** What is the computational overhead of DC-GRPO (direction extraction, periodic recalibration, per-batch activation extraction) compared to R3-only training? Is the added complexity justified even if results match the Continuous Probe?\n\n---\n\n### 7. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept) if best-tier results hold; 3 (Weak Reject) if median-tier results hold.**\n\n**Given the uncertainty, I lean toward 3 (Weak Reject).**\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a well-motivated problem (multi-turn sycophancy vs revision double-bind) and proposes a technically sound framework. The experimental design is comprehensive, with 15 experiments, appropriate baselines, and honest ablations. The turn-depth analysis showing DC-GRPO's advantage grows with dialogue depth is a strong finding regardless of tier.\n\nHowever, the fundamental contribution hinges on whether directional geometry provides value beyond continuous probe signals. The median-tier experiments (which I consider more likely given the base rates of representation-level discoveries) show this comparison is not significant. With only 3 seeds, even the best-tier significance at p=0.014 is fragile. If the advantage is merely from using continuous activation signals (which a simple logistic regression captures equally well), then the novelty is incremental: the RepE-based framing, the three-component reward design, and the recalibration mechanism add complexity without commensurate benefit over a simpler continuous probe approach.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) increasing to 5-10 seeds for primary comparisons, (2) clearly separating the evaluation framework contribution (RC-AUC, turn-depth analysis) from the method contribution (directional rewards), and (3) being transparent about the possibility that continuous signals, not directional geometry, drive the improvement. The median-tier version of the paper, which honestly acknowledges this, would be a more publishable contribution than the best-tier version, which over-claims directional advantage.\n\nFor a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate, the current evidence is insufficient to confidently accept. The risk that the core novelty claim dissolves upon replication with more seeds or the median experimental outcome is too high."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae7d15c37902555f6 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae7d15c37902555f6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 128726\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 135288</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab47c9b5-80cc-4b25-a0ea-3684b0ab8990","uuid":"345c01c7-0c76-4dca-a528-8e174eef6c1e","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:51:13.754Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **methodology soundness and experimental rigor**. Review the paper described below and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy Calibration: Representation-Level Rewards Break the Revision Double-Bind\n\n**Core idea:** Extract a linear direction d_syc from the policy model's activation space that discriminates sycophantic capitulation from genuine belief revision. Incorporate it into GRPO training as a three-component reward: R1 (directional projection), R2 (rank-based contrastive), R3 (NLI behavioral). The resulting DC-GRPO reduces sycophantic capitulation while preserving warranted revision. Also proposes RC-AUC (Revision-Capitulation AUC) as a composite evaluation metric.\n\n**Key results (Qwen3-8B + LoRA rank 64, 3 seeds):**\n- Calibration: d_syc at layer L18, AUROC 0.843 (95% CI [0.812, 0.874]), 200 style-controlled pairs\n- DC-GRPO: NoF 0.218, WRR 0.584, RC-AUC 0.738, MT-Bench 7.42\n- R3-only baseline: NoF 0.291, WRR 0.558, RC-AUC 0.641, MT-Bench 7.61\n- ACT-style: NoF 0.264, WRR 0.502, RC-AUC 0.648, MT-Bench 7.31\n- Binary Probe: NoF 0.239, WRR 0.572, RC-AUC 0.718, MT-Bench 7.47\n- Continuous Probe: NoF 0.224, WRR 0.579, RC-AUC 0.731, MT-Bench 7.44\n- Statistical significance: DC-GRPO vs R3-only: NoF p=0.021, RC-AUC p=0.008, WRR p=0.148. DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe: NoF p=0.381, RC-AUC p=0.318 (NOT significant).\n- Cross-model (Llama-3-8B): AUROC 0.778, RC-AUC 0.698, cross-model direction cosine 0.31\n\n**Ablations:**\n- R1-only: NoF 0.273, WRR 0.491 (below vanilla 0.537), RC-AUC 0.612\n- R1+R2 (no R3): NoF 0.252, WRR 0.483, surface inconsistency 8.7%\n- Reward weight sensitivity: 5 configs tested, RC-AUC ranges 0.661-0.738 (7.7pp span)\n- Direction stability: cosine drops from 1.0 to 0.76 over 8000 steps, held-out AUROC 0.843→0.791, recalibration threshold (0.70) never triggered\n- R1-R3 disagreement: 7.6% overall, increases with turn depth (5.8% turn 2 → 10.9% turn 4+)\n- Three-way direction: d_syc vs independent d_gen cosine = -0.72 (not -1.0), practical impact minimal (WRR +0.7pp)\n- Turn depth: DC-GRPO advantage over R3-only grows from 5.3pp (turn 2) to 9.4pp (turn 4+) on NoF\n\n**Experiment design reference (imagined experiments from review_context.yaml):**\n15 experiments were designed. The paper covers: exp-001 (calibration), exp-002 (DC-GRPO main), exp-003 (R3-only), exp-004 (ACT), exp-005 (full eval), exp-006 (direction stability), exp-007 (R1-R3 disagreement), exp-008 (binary probe), exp-009 (R1-only ablation), exp-010 (turn depth), exp-011 (R1+R2 no R3), exp-012 (cross-model), exp-013 (three-way direction), exp-014 (continuous probe), exp-015 (reward weight sensitivity). All 15 experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects with specific evidence from the paper:\n\n### 1. Methodology Soundness\n- Is the direction extraction (d_syc) method well-motivated and technically sound?\n- Is the three-component reward (R1, R2, R3) well-designed? Any concerns about interactions?\n- Is the RC-AUC metric well-defined and meaningful?\n- Is the recalibration mechanism adequately described and justified?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are baselines sufficient and fair? (same hyperparameters except reward)\n- Is 3 seeds sufficient for statistical claims? Which claims have adequate power?\n- The critical finding: DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe is NOT significant (p=0.381 NoF, p=0.318 RC-AUC). Does the paper handle this honestly?\n- Calibration data: 200 pairs sufficient? CI width 0.062 concerns?\n- Cross-model evaluation: only 2 models (both 8B), shorter training for Llama-3-8B (3000 vs 6500 steps)\n- Direction stability: AUROC drops to 0.791 by step 8000, approaching 0.80 gate. Is this a concern?\n\n### 3. Claims vs Evidence\n- Claim 1: activation space contains discriminative signal → supported (AUROC 0.843)\n- Claim 2: representation-level continuous rewards improve GRPO → supported, but the paper honestly notes the improvement comes from continuous signals, not directional geometry specifically\n- Claim 3: R1-R3 disagreement analysis → 7.6% disagreement, structured patterns, but acknowledged as insufficient for strong masked sycophancy claims\n- RC-AUC evaluation framework contribution → is this a sufficient standalone contribution?\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n- Are the tables and figures informative?\n- Is the paper well-organized?\n- Any missing details that harm reproducibility?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\nList the top 3-5 weaknesses with specific evidence.\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\nList 3-5 specific questions you would ask.\n\n### 7. Overall Assessment\nProvide a score on 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification. Consider this is for a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\nFormat your review with clear headers. Be specific and cite numbers from the paper.","agentId":"ae7d15c37902555f6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper across all three dream tiers. Let me produce the structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methodology Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n**Paper:** Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy Calibration: Representation-Level Rewards Break the Revision Double-Bind\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodology Soundness\n\n**Direction extraction (d_syc):** The method is well-motivated and technically sound in principle. Computing the mean difference vector from style-controlled contrastive pairs follows established RepE methodology (Zou et al., 2023). The style-control protocol (v2.1) is crucial and the paper documents its necessity through the progression from v1 (trivial confound, AUROC=1.0 at Layer 0) to v2 to v2.1. The mid-layer emergence pattern (L0-4 near random, peak at L18/L19) is consistent with prior work on linear representations and adds credibility.\n\nHowever, I have two concerns:\n\n(a) The direction is a simple mean difference vector, not PCA or any robust estimator. With 200 pairs, outliers could disproportionately influence the direction. The paper does not report any analysis of direction robustness to outlier removal or alternative extraction methods.\n\n(b) The evidence-strength stratification results raise a subtle issue. In the **median tier**, AUROC *decreases* with stronger evidence (weak 0.871 > medium 0.832 > strong 0.809), which makes intuitive sense: under strong evidence, genuine revision and sycophantic capitulation produce similar internal states because the model genuinely processes the evidence. But in the **best tier**, the ordering reverses (weak 0.863 < medium 0.894 < strong 0.921). These are contradictory predictions for the same experiment, and the correct ordering is theoretically ambiguous. The paper should justify why the observed ordering is expected rather than assuming one pattern and retroactively rationalizing it.\n\n**Three-component reward (R1, R2, R3):** The design is reasonable but under-specified in critical areas:\n\n- **R2 is vaguely described.** \"Rank-based contrastive\" is mentioned but the mathematical formulation is not provided. How exactly are within-batch responses ranked? What is the reward function over ranks? This is a reproducibility concern.\n- **Reward weight defaults (w1=0.3, w2=0.2, w3=0.5)** lack theoretical justification. The sensitivity analysis shows they work well empirically, but the initial selection appears arbitrary. The paper would be stronger if the choice were motivated, e.g., by a development set or by analysis of component magnitudes.\n- **R1-R3 interaction:** The ablation cascade (R3-only -> R1-only -> R1+R2 -> R1+R2+R3) is informative and well-structured. The non-trivial contribution of each component is convincingly demonstrated.\n\n**RC-AUC metric:** The concept of plotting WRR against (1-NoF) across thresholds is reasonable and addresses a real gap. However, the paper does not clearly specify what \"decision thresholds\" are swept to generate the curve. Is it a threshold on the total reward? On a confidence score? This definition is critical for the metric to be meaningful and reproducible. Without it, RC-AUC remains ambiguous.\n\n**Recalibration mechanism:** Every 500 steps with a 0.70 cosine threshold. The mechanism was never triggered in the best-case scenario (cosine stayed at 0.881). In the median case, cosine drops to 0.76 and is borderline. The 0.70 threshold seems arbitrary and may be too permissive. Had the paper used a 0.80 threshold, recalibration would have been triggered, potentially changing results. The choice is not well justified.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**Baselines:** The baseline set is comprehensive and well-chosen: Vanilla, R3-only (behavioral), ACT (undifferentiated activation consistency), Binary Probe, and Continuous Probe. All are trained with the same hyperparameters except the reward function, which is the correct experimental design. The inclusion of Continuous Probe is particularly important and was clearly added in response to internal review (D008).\n\n**3 seeds and statistical power:** This is the paper's most significant weakness. With only 3 seeds:\n- Paired t-tests have 2 degrees of freedom, making p-values unreliable.\n- Several claims are made based on p-values that are borderline. DC-GRPO vs R3-only NoF: p=0.021 (significant), WRR: p=0.148 (NOT significant). The paper claims DC-GRPO \"improves warranted revision rate by 12.2 pp\" but this improvement is not statistically significant.\n- Cohen's d calculations with n=3 have massive uncertainty.\n\nThe fact that the paper uses paired t-tests with n=3 and reports p-values to three decimal places creates a false impression of precision. With 3 observations, the t-distribution is very heavy-tailed, and small differences in the third seed can flip significance. I would require at least 5 seeds for the primary claims, preferably 10.\n\n**The critical DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe comparison:** This is the most important analysis in the paper and the numbers differ dramatically between tiers:\n- **Best tier:** DC-GRPO significantly outperforms Continuous Probe (NoF -7.9pp, p=0.014). The paper claims directional geometry is the primary driver.\n- **Median tier:** DC-GRPO does NOT significantly outperform Continuous Probe (NoF -0.6pp, p=0.381; RC-AUC +0.7pp, p=0.318). The improvement comes from continuous signals, not directional geometry.\n\nThe median-tier result is honest and informative, and the paper (in its median version) appropriately adjusts its claims. The best-tier version claims something stronger than what the median tier supports. The question is which outcome is more likely given the experimental design. With AUROC 0.843 on 200 pairs (median), a 7.9pp gap between directional projection and continuous probe probability (best) seems optimistic. The continuous probe and the directional projection both operate on the same underlying information (model activations at the same layer), so large divergence requires the geometric structure to carry substantial independent information.\n\n**Calibration data:** 200 pairs is adequate for direction extraction but marginal for strong statistical claims. The 95% CI width varies between 0.034 (best) and 0.062 (median). A CI width of 0.062 means the true AUROC could plausibly be anywhere from 0.812 to 0.874, which spans a meaningful range. The paper should acknowledge that 400-500 pairs would substantially strengthen confidence in the calibration signal.\n\n**Cross-model evaluation:** Testing on only 2 models (both 8B parameters) is a clear limitation. The Llama-3-8B training was shorter (3000 vs 6500-8000 steps), making direct comparison unfair. The cross-model direction cosine of 0.31-0.34 is low, confirming directions are model-specific. This weakens claims of generality. The paper should test on at least one model from a different parameter scale (e.g., 1.5B or 70B) to assess whether the phenomenon scales.\n\n**Direction stability:** AUROC drops from 0.843 to 0.791 (median) over 8000 steps, approaching the 0.80 gate. This is a genuine concern. If training were extended (e.g., to 12000 steps for better convergence), AUROC could drop below 0.80, at which point the R1 signal becomes unreliable. The paper does not investigate what happens when the recalibration threshold is actually triggered.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Claims vs Evidence\n\n**Claim 1 (discriminative signal):** Supported. AUROC 0.843 (median) at L18 with style-controlled data is a genuine finding. The mid-layer emergence pattern is convincing. However, \"discriminates\" is too strong for AUROC 0.843 with CI lower bound at 0.812. More appropriate: \"the activation space contains a moderately discriminative signal.\"\n\n**Claim 2 (DC-GRPO effectiveness):** Partially supported. DC-GRPO consistently outperforms R3-only and ACT. The critical question is whether the advantage is attributable to directional geometry or to continuous representation-level signals generally. The median-tier results (DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe not significant) suggest the latter. The best-tier claims of significant directional advantage (p=0.014) should be treated cautiously given n=3.\n\n**Claim 3 (R1-R3 disagreement):** The paper appropriately downgrades this to an analysis subsection rather than a formal contribution. The disagreement rate varies between 7.6% (median) and 14.2% (best). The structured patterns (increase with turn depth, concentration in medium evidence) are informative regardless of the absolute rate. The paper does not provide human validation of whether flagged cases are truly \"masked sycophancy\" versus noise.\n\n**RC-AUC contribution:** As an evaluation metric, RC-AUC fills a genuine gap. However, the metric needs clearer formal specification (what thresholds are swept) and external validation (correlation with human judgments). Without these, it risks the \"strongest results on self-invented metric\" criticism.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n\nThe paper is generally well-organized with a logical progression: direction extraction, main results, ablations, analysis. Tables are informative with appropriate standard deviations reported. The Related Work section correctly positions the work within RepE/CCS and distinguishes it from CRAFT and ACT.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The abstract in the best tier over-claims (\"AUROC 0.891,\" \"7.9 pp improvement with p=0.014 against Continuous Probe\") while the median tier is more honest. The actual experimental results will determine which is appropriate.\n- R2 reward component is inadequately formalized. The \"rank-based contrastive\" description is insufficient for reproduction.\n- The paper does not report the computational overhead of DC-GRPO vs R3-only training (direction extraction, recalibration every 500 steps, activation extraction per batch). This is practically important.\n- No human evaluation of any outputs. WRR and NoF depend on automated classification of whether revision is \"warranted\" or capitulation is \"sycophantic.\" The accuracy of these classifiers is not validated.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Top Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Insufficient seeds for statistical claims (Critical).** All statistical tests use n=3 seeds with paired t-tests (2 df). This is severely underpowered. The WRR comparison between DC-GRPO and R3-only (p=0.148) already fails significance. With more seeds, some currently \"significant\" results may fail, and the critical DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe comparison (which determines the core novelty claim) is extremely sensitive to noise. The p=0.014 in the best tier vs p=0.381 in the median tier illustrates this fragility.\n\n**W2. Directional geometry vs continuous signals not clearly separated.** The median-tier results show DC-GRPO and Continuous Probe are statistically indistinguishable. This means the paper's title claim (\"representation-level rewards break the double-bind\") is more accurately about continuous activation-space signals, not specifically about directional projection. If the final experiments confirm the median scenario, the novelty is substantially reduced: a logistic regression probability works equally well.\n\n**W3. Limited model diversity.** Only two 8B models from different families, with unequal training budgets. No exploration of parameter scale effects. Cross-model direction cosine of 0.31 suggests the specific findings may not generalize.\n\n**W4. RC-AUC under-specified.** The paper's proposed metric lacks formal definition of the threshold sweep procedure and has no correlation with human judgments. The strongest results (RC-AUC 0.738 vs 0.641) are on this self-defined metric.\n\n**W5. No human validation.** Neither the calibration data labels (sycophantic vs genuine) nor the evaluation metrics (NoF, WRR) are validated against human judgments. The entire pipeline assumes the automated classifiers are correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The DC-GRPO vs Continuous Probe comparison is the crux of the paper's novelty claim. With only 3 seeds, what is the statistical power to detect a 7.9pp difference at alpha=0.05? Please report power analysis and consider whether 5+ seeds would change conclusions.\n\n**Q2.** The evidence-strength stratification of AUROC has a theoretically ambiguous direction (should strong evidence yield higher or lower AUROC?). Can you provide a formal argument for why the observed ordering is expected, rather than post-hoc rationalization?\n\n**Q3.** What is the formal definition of R2 (rank-based contrastive reward)? The current description is insufficient for reproduction. Please provide the mathematical formulation.\n\n**Q4.** How would you respond to the possibility that a Continuous Probe achieves equivalent results (as in the median scenario)? Would you adjust the contribution claims to focus on the evaluation framework and multi-turn analysis rather than directional geometry?\n\n**Q5.** What is the computational overhead of DC-GRPO (direction extraction, periodic recalibration, per-batch activation extraction) compared to R3-only training? Is the added complexity justified even if results match the Continuous Probe?\n\n---\n\n### 7. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept) if best-tier results hold; 3 (Weak Reject) if median-tier results hold.**\n\n**Given the uncertainty, I lean toward 3 (Weak Reject).**\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a well-motivated problem (multi-turn sycophancy vs revision double-bind) and proposes a technically sound framework. The experimental design is comprehensive, with 15 experiments, appropriate baselines, and honest ablations. The turn-depth analysis showing DC-GRPO's advantage grows with dialogue depth is a strong finding regardless of tier.\n\nHowever, the fundamental contribution hinges on whether directional geometry provides value beyond continuous probe signals. The median-tier experiments (which I consider more likely given the base rates of representation-level discoveries) show this comparison is not significant. With only 3 seeds, even the best-tier significance at p=0.014 is fragile. If the advantage is merely from using continuous activation signals (which a simple logistic regression captures equally well), then the novelty is incremental: the RepE-based framing, the three-component reward design, and the recalibration mechanism add complexity without commensurate benefit over a simpler continuous probe approach.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) increasing to 5-10 seeds for primary comparisons, (2) clearly separating the evaluation framework contribution (RC-AUC, turn-depth analysis) from the method contribution (directional rewards), and (3) being transparent about the possibility that continuous signals, not directional geometry, drive the improvement. The median-tier version of the paper, which honestly acknowledges this, would be a more publishable contribution than the best-tier version, which over-claims directional advantage.\n\nFor a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate, the current evidence is insufficient to confidently accept. The risk that the core novelty claim dissolves upon replication with more seeds or the median experimental outcome is too high."}],"totalDurationMs":135288,"totalTokens":128726,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22222,"cache_read_input_tokens":102826,"output_tokens":3677,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":22222},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3677,"cache_read_input_tokens":102826,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22222,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":22222,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
